row  is sort of waxes and the bottom row are  more oils on the right side of the  screen is a vape pen and we've tested a  quite a wide range of these types of  concentrates we also tested a wide range  of other types of concentrates and  there's various pills and powders and  oils this is a representative selection  of what we tested  so the first step in the analysis is to  dissolve the sample so we found after  doing a dissolution test that methanol  was the most effective solvent and for  nearly all of the different types of  concentrates we found methanol was very  good at dissolving all the cannabinoids  and all and all the solid material and  so we use this as the first step of our  pesticide analysis for concentrates  and really the cannabis concentrates are  much cleaner already than the dry flour  material so we don't use a cleanup  procedure for the concentrates simply a  deleted chute  we run all the samples with a internal  standard and we also carried out a  single point standardization experiment  to verify the absence of matrix effects  on these on these samples so shown in  the picture are a batch of 50 different  concentrates you can see a wide range of  color that we find depending how much  chlorophyll remains in the sample as  well as the other Co extracted  components of the sample  so after extraction and filtering and we  run by the same lc-ms analysis we can  show the same validated method for  cannabis concentrates as well and on  this slide we're showing the single  standard single point standard edition  results showing that we are detecting  spiked pesticides as we expected for  most of the analytes  and on this slide we're showing the  results of our market survey for  cannabis concentrates in this case we  use 48 samples from the Denver market  and we're finding a very high positivity  rate and we think this is due to the  fact that in the cannabis concentrates  the pesticides are actually concentrated  more than than they were in the flower  and then by that fact they are going to  be above the required action limit on  each in each state so it may be more  likely for cannabis concentrates to test  positive for pesticides than the flower  material so in this case we're showing  over 80% positive rate and again paper  no meat oxide pyrethrins or the most  common also michael butanol and some of  the new Nikita knives are also detected  as well  and for comparison in Spokane we looked  at about a hundred samples and this is  again a similar selection of cannabis  concentrates and we found a similar  positive detection rate that we found in  Denver that's over 80 percent testing  positive and again the same pesticides  testing positive that we found in Denver  and I want to call attention especially  to several the several important  compounds in this list which are not  required to be monitored by the states  in particular carbon design so carbon is  emisn antifungal that made the headlines  a few years ago being found in oranges  and orange juice and it has an  antifungal activity so it's no surprise  to find this on cannabis but it's not  actually required to be monitored in any  of the states that we visited or in  Canada either but we found it in about  10% of our samples so this could reflect  a trend among growers to switch to  unlisted pesticides  and  to show the method robustness over a  two-week period and almost a thousand  injections of samples we show that the  instrument performance is very good  and we can measure that by retention  time by peak with iron ratio and signal  intensity and it shows that this method  is well suited to high throughput and  also long-term analysis of pesticides in  cannabis even with these very  complicated samples  so to conclude we found that pesticides  were used in cannabis and fairly widely  we found that our method gave a very  good result in terms of being efficient  to use and sensitive to detect low  levels of pesticides we found that the  cannabis concentrates could be analyzed  by just simple dilute and Shu method and  we found very good lcms robust  performance and we also found that there  could be growers switching to pesticides  that are not in any of the state  monitoring lists so it's important for  States to keep aware of new pesticides  that are not currently being monitored  so inclusion I want to thank our  collaborators all the work that we've  done has been in collaboration with  state certified laboratories since the  product is federally illegal so  especially we worked with members of  trace analytics in Spokane Washington as  well as members from other organizations  as well providing consumables and we  also want to thank your collaborators at  Nordic  and I want to thank all of you in the  audience for your attention and I'd be  happy to answer any questions you may  have  thank you dr. doll for that informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question  drop-down box located on the far left of  your presentation window type your  question into the box that appears on  your screen and click the send button  once there's many of your questions as  we have time for so let's get started  our first question is can a smaller  sample amount be used and how are the  samples collected  well that's a good question in the  cannabis industry there's a strong  pressure to use smaller samples because  the product is very valuable but in on  the other hand we have to make sure that  the sample is representative and so  typically what the states require is  that sample collector represents the lab  and collects a sample and a  statistically sound sampling procedure  usually that means going to a production  lot and selecting a small sub sample  from a randomly assigned part of the  batch so if we had a 15-pound batch this  collector will select a small sample  from several different places throughout  the batch to ensure that a  representative sample is selected and  depending on the state's rules the  sample might range from 10 or 20 grams  for example and from that larger sample  we would then select an even smaller sub  sample so in the presentation we used a  1 gram sample for the pesticide analysis  in principle there's nothing stopping us  from using a smaller sample because the  instruments are able to detect  pesticides from a smaller amount our for  to to get a good representativeness  typically we use a 1 gram sub sample  and our next question is can I test for  potency be done in the same analysis as  the pesticides  so the potency measurement is quite a  different measurement than the  pesticides the instrumentation is  capable of doing both pesticides and  potency but because of the large  difference in concentration we usually  would do a different dilution to do the  potency measurement so the pesticides  might be present in concentrations as  low as five or ten parts per billion  whereas the cannabinoids might be in  concentrations that are more of you know  several percent up to 20 percent and so  typically even though the  instrumentation could make the  measurement we'd usually need to dilute  the sample more for the potency  measurement and due to some other  factors such as the ease of calibration  we usually recommend running potency by  an LCU v and then dedicating the lc/ms  towards the pesticide measurement  doctors all it looks like we have time  for one more question can you detect  pesticides that are not on the state  required monitoring list so with our  measurement techniques the typical  workflow is to use the mrm mode for  detection and in MRI mode we're  typically starting with a targeted  pesticide list and in our presentation  we are covering a larger list of  compounds than is required by any of the  states and we can certainly add many  more pesticides to those monitoring  lists and in fact we found pesticides in  cannabis samples that were not actually  required to be measured by the states  and this could reflect growers switching  to different pesticides so we certainly  can expand the monitoring list beyond  what the states require  I would like to once again thank dr.  Dahle for his presentation I would also  like to thank laborat for making today's  educational webcast possible before we  go I want to let everyone know that  today's webcast will be available for  on-demand viewing through June of 2018  you will receive an email from laberd's  letting you know when this webcast will  be available for replay please share  that announcement with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us and we hope to see you again soon  goodbye
 

